'''temperamental''' (tem″pėr-a̤-men′tal), ''a.'' [< ''temperament'' + ''-al.''] Of or pertaining to temperament.

{{smaller block|Few overcome their ''temperamental'' inclinations.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Christ. Mor., iii. 22.}}
Undoubtedly there is a ''temperamental'' courage, a warlike blood, which loves a fight, does not feel itself except in a quarrel, as one sees in wasps, or ants, or cocks, or cats.
{{right|''Emerson'', Courage.}}}}

'''temperamentally''' (tem″pėr-a̤-men′tal-i), ''adv.'' In temperament; as regards temperament. ''The Century'', XX. 89.

'''temperance''' (tem′pėr-ans), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''temperaunce;'' < ME. ''temperance'', < OF. ''temperance, temprance'', F. ''tempérance'' = Pr. ''tempransa'' = Sp. ''templanza, temperancia'' = Pg. ''temperança'' = It. ''tempranza'', < L. ''temperantia'', moderation, sobriety, < ''temperan(t-)s'', ppr. of ''temperare'', moderate, temper: see ''temperant.''] 1. Moderation; the observance of moderation; temperateness.

{{smaller block|True sentiment is emotion ripened by a slow ferment of the mind and qualified to an agreeable ''temperance'' by that taste which is the conscience of polite society.
{{right|''Lowell'', Among my Books, 1st ser., p. 366.}}}}

{{smaller block|Particularly—(''a'') Habitual moderation in regard to the indulgence of the natural appetites and passions; restrained or moderate indulgence; abstinence from all violence or excess, from inordinate or unseasonable indulgence, or from the use or pursuit of anything injurious to moral or physical well-being; sobriety; frugality: as, ''temperance'' in eating and drinking; ''temperance'' in the indulgence of joy or grief; in a narrower sense, moderation in the use of alcoholic liquors, as beverages; or, in a still narrower sense as used by its advocates, entire abstinence from such liquors: in this sense also used attributively: as, a ''temperance'' society; a ''temperance'' hotel; a ''temperance''}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
{{smaller block|It [the island] must needs be of subtle, tender, and delicate ''temperance''.
{{right|''Shak.'', Tempest, ii. 1. 42.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Temperance hotel''', a hotel in which no intoxicating liquors are supplied to the guests or kept for sale.—'''Temperance movement''', a social or political movement having for its object the restriction or abolition of the use of alcoholic liquors as beverages.—'''Temperance society''', an association formed for the purpose of suppressing drunkenness. The basis on which these associations have been formed has been that of an engagement on the part of each member to abstain from the excessive or habitual use of intoxicating liquors. But, since the most strictly limited use of intoxicants as beverages is condemned by many social reformers, this name has been very generally applied to, or assumed by, associations which are more correctly designated ''total-abstinence societies''.='''Syn.''' 1. (''a'') ''Abstinence'', ''Sobriety'', etc. See ''abstemiousness''.}}

'''temperancy†''' (tem′pėr-an-si), ''n.'' [As ''temperance'' (see ''-cy'').] Temperance.

'''temperant†''', ''a.'' [ME. *''temperant'', ''temporaunt'', < OF. ''temperant'', F. ''tempérant'' = Sp. It. ''temperante'' = Pg. ''temperante'', < L. ''temperan''(''t-'')''s'', ppr. of ''temperare'', moderate, temper: see ''temper'', ''temperate''.] Moderate; temperate.

{{smaller block|Northwarde in places hote, in places colde
Southward, and ''temporaunt'' in Est and West.
{{right|''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 5.}}}}

'''temperate''' (tem′pėr-āt), ''a.'' [< ME. ''temperate'' = F. ''tempéré'' = Sp. ''templado'' = Pg. ''temprado'', ''temperado'', < L. ''temperatus'', pp. of ''temperare'': see ''temper''. Cf. ''tempre''.] 1. Moderate; showing moderation; not excessive, lavish, or inordinate.

{{smaller block|And what you fancy to bestow on him,
Be not too lavish, use a ''temperate'' bounty.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Staple of News, ii.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
{{smaller block|Let not the government of the plantation depend upon too many counsellors and undertakers in the country that planteth, but upon a ''temperate'' number.
{{right|''Bacon'', Plantations (ed. 1887).}}}}

{{smaller block|Rain-scented eglantine
Gave ''temperate'' sweets to that well-wooing Sun.
{{right|''Keats'', Endymion, i.}}}}

{{smaller block|In these [early French Pointed capitals] alone is perfect structural adaptation joined with the highest and most ''temperate'' grace. ''C. H. Moore'', Gothic Architecture, p. 208.}}

{{smaller block|More especially—(''a'') Moderate as regards the indulgence of the appetites or desires; abstemious; sober; continent: as, ''temperate'' in eating; ''temperate'' habits.}}

{{smaller block|He that is ''temperate'' fleeth pleasures voluptuous.
{{right|''Sir T. Elyot'', The Governour, iii. 19.}}}}

{{smaller block|If he be insatiable in plunder and revenge, shall we pass it by because in meat and drink he is ''temperate?''
{{right|''Macaulay'', Conversations between Cowley and Milton.}}}}

{{smaller block|(''b'') Not violent or extravagant in the use of language; calm; measured; dispassionate: as, a ''temperate'' discourse.}}

{{smaller block|The sentence of the board of generals which condemned André remains, and no document could be more ''temperate'' or better reasoned. ''Lecky'', Eng. in 18th Cent., xiv.}}

2. Not swayed by passion; calm; self-contained; self-restrained; not extreme in opinions.

{{smaller block|Whanne the Sowdon had hard hym euery dele,
Withynne a while he was right ''temperate''.
{{right|''Generydes'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 1661.}}}}

{{smaller block|The ''temperate'' man deliteth in nothynge contrarye to reason. ''Sir T. Elyot'', The Governour, iii. 20.}}

{{smaller block|Who can be wise, amazed, ''temperate'' and furious,
Loyal and neutral in a moment?
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, ii. 3. 114.}}}}

{{smaller block|Peace, lady! pause, or be more ''temperate''.
{{right|''Shak.'', K. John, ii. 1. 195.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
