3. Proceeding from temperance; moderate.

{{smaller block|He [Richard Baxter] belonged to the mildest and most ''temperate'' section of the Puritan body.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., iv.}}}}

4. Moderate in respect of temperature; not liable to excessive heat or cold; mild; specifically, noting certain zones of the earth's surface.

{{smaller block|When ''temperate'' heat offends not with extremes.
{{right|''Dekker and Ford'', Sun's Darling, iv. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|They said they came to an Island of a very ''temperate'' Air, where they look'd upon it as the greatest Indecency in the World to cover their Bodies.
{{right|''N. Bailey'', tr. of Colloquies of Erasmus, I. 370.}}}}

5. In ''music'', same as ''tempered''.—{{smaller|'''Temperate zones''', the parts of the earth lying between the tropics and the polar circles, where the climate is cooler than between the tropics and warmer than within the polar circles. The ''north temperate zone'' is the space included between the tropic of Cancer and the arctic circle; and the ''south temperate zone'', that between the tropic of Capricorn and the antarctic circle. See ''zone''.}}={{smaller|'''Syn. 1-4.''' ''Moderate, Temperate''. See ''moderate''.}}

'''temperate†''' (tem′pėr-āt), ''v. t.'' [< L. ''temperatus'', pp. of ''temperare'', modify, temper: see ''temper'', ''v.''] To temper; moderate.

{{smaller block|In heaven and earth this power beauty hath—
It inflames temperance, and ''temp'rates'' wrath.
{{right|''Marston and Barksted'', Insatiate Countess, i.}}}}

{{smaller block|Sometimes ''temperated'' by the comfortable winds, to which it lies open.
{{right|''Sandys'', Travailes, p. 178.}}}}

'''temperately''' (tem′pėr-ạ̄t-li), ''adv.'' In a temperate manner or degree. {{smaller|(''a'') Moderately; not excessively.}}
